lo nu})
>
> I strongly believe that kakne2 should be a ka-abstraction.

Me too, although I don't have very strong feelings about it. I just=20
think it makes more sense.

The story is this: When I started translating Oz, I was still using {nu}=20
with predicates like {troci}, but some way in I finally made the switch=20
to {ka}. I thought I had replaced most {kakne lo nu} by now, but=20
apparently there still are a few left.

It is good that you bring this up, however, because I want people to=20
know that the intended form is indeed the {kakne lo ka} one, and=20
anything else in kakne2 is a left-over from before and should be=20
considered a typo.

I will list a few other predicates that I now thing should not use {nu},=20
but which I used to use with {nu} (please feel free to add cases where=20
these are used with {nu} to the list of errata spreadsheet):
kakne, troci, snada, cpedu, minde, zukte, nitcu, bilga, certu, ...

(keep in mind, though, that I do make intentional use of polymorphism=20
sometimes)

> By the way, there are other uses of {k=C4=81kne} that are more subtly rel=
ated
> to the issue. E.g.,
>
> (10) {g=C4=81snu ro lo se k=C4=81kne be se va'u do}

Yes. Same thing as before, though this sort of case is why I kept on=20
using {nu} with the likes of {kakne} and {troci}. I thought it was too=20
useful to be able to say {lo se troci cu rinka lo nu morsi}. I finally=20
got persuaded to change my mind, though, so this is no longer a sentence=20
I would defend as being entirely correct.

